1. What is Agile Methodology?
   Agile is a software development methodology in which we develop and deliver software in small, incremental iterations
   instead of developing the entire application at once.

   It focuses on:
   Continuous customer feedback.
   Frequent delivery of working software.
   Collaboration between developers, testers, and business teams.
   Adapting to changing requirements.
   Continuous testing and improvement.

   "Agile is an iterative and incremental software development methodology. We divide the project into smaller
   iterations called sprints, where each sprint delivers a working piece of functionality. It encourages continuous
   customer feedback, collaboration, frequent releases, and flexibility to accommodate changing requirements."

2. How does Agile work?
   Agile development is divided into small iterations called Sprints. A sprint usually lasts 1–2 weeks, sometimes 3–4
   weeks.

